::ALG2_U01_L05_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 6) by (x + 2). {
=x + 3 #Correct. x squared plus 5x plus 6 factors as (x + 2)(x + 3), so the quotient is x + 3.
~x + 2 #This is the divisor, not the quotient.
~x + 5 #Multiplying (x + 2)(x + 5) gives x squared plus 7x plus 10, not the dividend.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 3 #This does not have the correct degree for dividing a quadratic by a linear expression.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6) by (x - 2). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 1 #This copies coefficients instead of completing the division.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 3 #Correct. Synthetic division with 2 gives quotient coefficients 1, -2, -3 and remainder 0.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 3 #This uses the wrong sign pattern in the quotient.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 3 #The constant term should be -3, not 3.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>When using synthetic division for a divisor of x + 4, which value should be placed outside the synthetic division setup? {
~4 #This is the sign in the divisor, but synthetic division uses the value that makes x + 4 equal 0.
=-4 #Correct. x + 4 is x - (-4), so the synthetic value is -4.
~0 #The divisor is not x, so 0 is not the correct synthetic value.
~1 #This value would match x - 1, not x + 4.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (2x<sup>3</sup> + 3x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 4) by (x + 2). {
~2x<sup>2</sup> - x - 3 #This is only the quotient and leaves out the nonzero remainder.
=2x<sup>2</sup> - x - 3 + 10/(x + 2) #Correct. The quotient is 2x squared minus x minus 3, and the remainder is 10.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 7x - 19 #This uses the wrong synthetic value and does not match the dividend.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 5 + 4/(x + 2) #This copies coefficients instead of dividing.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial is divided by x - 3 and the remainder is 0. What conclusion is correct? {
~x - 3 is not a factor. #A zero remainder means the divisor divides evenly.
=x - 3 is a factor. #Correct. A divisor with remainder 0 is a factor of the polynomial.
~The quotient must be 0. #The remainder is 0, but the quotient does not have to be 0.
~The polynomial has degree 0. #The degree of the polynomial is not determined by the remainder alone.
}
